      <workList xml:id="workDesc_2e25fb45">
         <work xml:id="work_id5224c83f">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_c4693c13">B4:001</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id1c96ee4d" xml:lang="la">Kyrie</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_6af9f3ea">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_id2856ec65">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <history xml:id="history_idea006cf1">
               <p xml:id="idm69">The work, which presumably is lost, is mentioned in <persName xml:id="idm70" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Philipp Kirnberger</persName>’s <rend xml:id="idm71" fontstyle="italic">Die Kunst des reinen Satzes</rend> (1779). Kirnberger had various autograph manuscript scores of Scheibe’s in his possession which he might have acquired from Scheibe’s estate after 1776. Though there are entries in the Breitkopf catalogues revealing that Scheibe composed Magnificat and Sanctus sections (SchW B4:002–B4:007), there are, however, no indications suggesting that they also included at least one Kyrie of Scheibe’s in their stock of manuscripts for sale. It should be noted that it was rare for Lutheran churches to perform Kyrie and Credo movements at church services; hence it seems unlikely that the work would have been performed at Neukirche in Leipzig. Kirnberger uses the Kyrie as an example of how some composers employed a three-part framework to build a composition of nineteen parts. It has not been possible to locate the manuscript and it is not part of  the 'Kirnberger Collection' of the Amalien-Bibliothek which he founded.</p>
            </history>
